00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.650
be and maybe what
the Data Jam is.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:29.430
- Danielle, do you
want to give it a shot?

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:30.620
- So the whole--

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:32.780
so we have a
couple of different community of

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.010
practices at Biogen,

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:37.970
one of them that Anuja and I
lead is the Data and Analytics. And really,

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:41.330
the whole goal was just to
get people across Biogen that are

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.445
passionate about data
and analytics together,

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.490
just to learn the different
ways that we work across the

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:50.100
different groups, find areas
of opportunity and collaboration,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:50.780
as well as network.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:52.400
And really,

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.265
the way the Data Jam came
about is things have been pretty--

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.010
has been pretty
interesting at work.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:02.490
And so we wanted to try to find
a way to have a little bit of

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.020
fun, try to get
people together.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.110
I think Anuja had mentioned she
loves getting people together. So do I.

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:08.350
So we decided,

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:11.490
why not try to throw together
a Data Jam and something that we

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.130
thought would just be something small
and fun turned out to be

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.445
something, actually, very big and
really show the passion that

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:19.680
people at Biogen has for data.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:23.330
I don't know if Dan and Anuja
if you want to add on to that.

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:26.745
- That was pretty good
from my perspective. I think anything

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.050
that's successful, I think,
always happens something like

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.610
this. Danielle comes to me--

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.840
Danielle and Anuja come to me
in one meeting and say, hey,

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:38.430
I think we should do it like
a data competition. We're like, all right, sure,

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.460
let's put that together. Let's
see what that looks like.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:42.110
And the

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:44.370
day you send it out--

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:45.860
like

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.800
I was hoping myself, 30
people would show up.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.530
I thought if we had 30
people, we'd have like--

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.980
we'd have put five people on
a team. You have six teams.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.600
You'd have a good
competition with six teams.

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.410
I think in the first
hour, 50 people signed up,

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:04.230
and then we just went into
panic mode of how we're going

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:05.200
to organize this,

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.530
which we could also talk
about because there's a lot there.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:12.360
But really, I think it
starts on something small,

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.360
and then we just had such
a great team together to be able

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.075
to put something like this
together. It really worked out well.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:18.875
-

00:07:18.875 --> 00:07:23.020
So I think a lot of people
have ideas for things like this or

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:26.140
just ideas in general
that they'll bring to meetings,

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:28.880
but you all actually
made it happen.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.880
How did you go from just an
idea for something to making it this real-life

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:35.010
event?

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:37.650
- Anuja, do you
want to take that?

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:38.720
- Yeah, I can take that.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:39.980
I think

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:43.610
actually executing to
the point of getting

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:46.340
a data set and sending out

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:50.160
a form for sign-ups was
fairly straightforward.

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.520
We had a couple of ideas
of data sets that we could use.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:57.330
And so we landed on one and
then just decided to go for it.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:00.065
We had enough information to
execute, and we sent it out.

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:03.770
Where the challenge came in
was when we saw the rate of

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:05.120
sign-ups because people--

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.730
we realized, we
knew on some level,

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:11.060
but we really saw that
people love data and are passionate

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:12.650
about it and want to work

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.175
on a hackathon of sorts
or a Data Jam of sorts.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:18.770
So I will say that we--

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:23.210
it got a little challenging when
we hit 200 to 250 because

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:26.375
our previous plan for how
we were going to run it

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:30.930
wasn't really going to
work anymore, given the numbers.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:33.539
And so there was a lot
of planning on the fly.

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:36.390
So when we saw how
many people there were,

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.909
we decided let's do two
rounds instead of one.

00:08:38.909 --> 00:08:40.710
And so it was part
of the excitement,

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:44.340
but it also was part of
the challenge of figuring out how

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:49.890
to accommodate the growing
number of participants. So yeah.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:50.850
- I think part of it, too,

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:53.460
was that we actually had
support from our leadership to

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:56.235
actually go forth and do
this, and that helped a lot.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:57.930
I think part of it, too,

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.450
was the fact that there has
been a lot of eyes just on data

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:02.290
in general for many
different reasons.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.710
So I think that also helped.
But a lot of it, too,

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:08.230
is us just trying to figure
out how to even pull it off.

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:09.000
And then we did,

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:11.495
to be completely honest.

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:12.425
-

00:09:12.425 --> 00:09:14.600
And I might clarify what
Danielle says because it's not

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.010
like we were given a task

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:18.080
to go run a data jam.

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:20.840
At some point, we just
had to make a decision,

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:23.600
like Rachel says. There are ideas
all the time, right? How do you

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:25.235
do it?

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:28.400
There's a little bit of
a leap of faith there.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:29.420
I think you just--

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:32.780
what's the worst that can
happen? It's no one signs up,

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:34.130
and we just go back
to our day jobs.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:36.470
What's the best thing
that can happen?

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:38.180
It's a whole bunch
of people sign up.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.325
So there is just
a little bit of

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:43.740
boldness from this team to
be able to say, all right,

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:46.055
let's do it, let's give it
a shot and see what happens.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:48.530
- I think a lot of it
too is we just asked.

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:49.630
It was like,

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:50.915
there's no downside

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:54.380
to expect. And so I think a
lot of it was we just asked.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:55.085
And it seemed like

00:09:55.700 --> 00:09:57.020
we were able to get
a lot of things,

00:09:57.650 --> 00:09:59.045
a lot of yeses, actually.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:00.790
- That's great.

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:04.910
I've been thinking about doing
something kind of like that at

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:06.620
Posit so that we could

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.290
mix people across
all different teams,

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:12.260
like Customer Success and some
of the open source package

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:16.340
developers, to work on a
common problem together.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.920
And it's just been like an idea
floating around in my head.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:23.750
So I was just curious how
you went and made it actually happen.

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:29.810
But I was wondering, for
the teams, were people mixed across

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:33.080
different parts of the
company or could people be--

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.420
it could be a group of whoever
wanted to join together?

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:39.500
- We decided to randomize it.
And so we had people sign up.

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:44.060
And part of the sign up
was asking what their capabilities were.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:48.020
So is it that they've
never really coded before? Are they

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:52.040
experts or gurus? Do they
fall somewhere in the middle?

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:53.770
So we

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:55.850
defined four different levels

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:58.950
of competency and

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:02.824
had people describe
or self-identify

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:05.030
within one of those four.

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:08.330
And then what we tried to
do is when we did randomize teams,

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:11.310
we tried to kind of
spread the capabilities out. Honestly,

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:12.720
most people fell in the middle.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:16.350
But we didn't want to put
all the experts together,

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.640
but also didn't want to have
a team that was all novices.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:22.800
And so the Data Jam, of
course, was about data,

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:26.380
but another kind of primary element
was for people to meet each other.

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:29.410
And so we didn't want people
to just stay within their teams.

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:32.130
So this was an opportunity
to network outside as well.

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:32.630
- Yeah,

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:34.770
it was wild, the teams--

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:36.210
because we don't know
any of the people. Biogen

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:37.665
is a big company.

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:40.870
But the teams end
up wild, multiple

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:45.390
time zones, people in
like Buenos Aires, and China,

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:48.120
and Switzerland, and US.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.430
And they gave them like three
weeks to go analyze some data.

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:52.320
They don't know each other.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.630
They work in wildly
different departments,

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:57.840
some in manufacturing, some
in sales, some in R&amp;amp;D.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:11:59.690
So

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:01.770
like you're saying, Rachel,

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:06.270
a big part is giving
those people, force them into something.

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:09.900
And I feel like you get a lot
of benefit when people are

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:13.470
forced to problem-solve
together because that--

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:14.985
it creates a little bit of--

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:17.910
I don't know, a little
bit of conflict.

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:19.110
And when you get that conflict,

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.720
then you get people's different
opinions coming out and the

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:24.750
different viewpoints, then you
build on something together. So

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:27.870
I think that part was
really wildly successful.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:30.495
And people had a good time
meeting people across the org.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:32.855
- Yeah, thank you. Alan,

00:12:32.855 --> 00:12:34.830
I see you asked a
question in the chat here.

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:35.940
Do you want to jump in?

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:38.040
ALAN: Yeah, sure.
Hey, everybody.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:39.730
I think Dan was kind
of just speaking to

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:41.175
part of the question,

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:44.160
noting that folks had several
weeks to put something

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:45.885
together, and they were
kind of all over the place.

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:49.770
I'd really like to know more
about what did they make?

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.860
And how much structure did you
give them to lead into that?

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:55.650
Was this an internal
and external data set?

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:57.960
And did you give them a
bunch of specific problems,

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.120
or did you say, hey,
here's a pile of data,

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:02.640
find something fun and
interesting and make something?

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:05.580
And I'm curious about the array
of things that they made and

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:06.560
what the sharing--

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:10.590
with a great big set of 200
people sharing that back out at

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:12.400
scale is also
really challenging.

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:13.815
So how did that--

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:17.575
how did this conducting of
the rest of the thing unfold?

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:19.480
There's a whole bunch there.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:21.190
Whatever is kind of
interesting to speak to,

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:23.620
I'd love to hear
about it. Thanks.

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:24.160
- Maybe, Danielle.

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:26.560
- Yeah, so we actually--

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:28.930
so we started with
an internal data set.

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:31.250
It was a survey that was
done a few years ago,

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:33.700
specifically around
data maturity at Biogen.

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:37.780
And so we had a data
set that was easy,

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:40.990
somewhat easy to
use and understand.

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:43.315
And then the way we set it
up was we gave the teams

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:45.310
a few weeks to

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:49.030
analyze the data set in
whatever fashion or whatever

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:50.725
type of tool they
wanted to use.

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:54.740
And the idea was to
generate insights.

00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:56.650
There are a few key
questions that we had asked,

00:13:56.650 --> 00:13:58.255
but other than that,
it was pretty much open

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:01.330
and then present it
not in PowerPoint.

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:04.750
So the challenge here was to
not use PowerPoint and to be creative.

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:09.540
And so what we did was
we had a few rounds of

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:13.000
battle rounds. We

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:15.850
posed it as like a
Eurovision, The

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:18.730
Voice type of
head-to-head knockout battle

00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:21.040
rounds where we had groups of,

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.110
I think it was
five or six teams,

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:25.990
and then they would vote for
the one team to move forward to

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:30.790
the finale. So at the end, we
had, I think, 22 teams down to

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:31.940
five teams.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:35.170
Is it five teams, Anuja? Yeah.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:36.852
- Four or five, yeah.

00:14:36.852 --> 00:14:38.440
- Four or five, yeah.
It's kind of a blur there,

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:40.015
but we had it down
to five teams. And so

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:44.530
after that, we had like the
big finale where it was--

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:46.600
something that we did in
one of our auditoriums,

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:48.250
and we had all the
teams come present.

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:50.590
We were actually able
to get our executive,

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:53.230
some executive committee
members to come and do some

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:56.320
openings and some closing.
And we got our CEO there.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:15:01.030
It was a really big event where
we then had people present in a Shark

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:05.950
Tank-style pitch about the
data sets and what they

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:07.090
would recommend to

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:11.260
better to have better data
stewardship and different types

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:13.030
of strategies with
data at Biogen.

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:15.355
And then we crowned
a winner at the end.

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:18.130
We had some
trophies and medals.

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:21.220
- I love how you're mixing
all these reality shows like

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:23.560
The Voice and
Shark Tank. And

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:28.130
I see Jesse asked a
question in the chat.

00:15:28.130 --> 00:15:29.840
And I see a star
there, so I'll read it.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:31.550
But the question was,

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:35.260
did the competition happen
during work hours? And if so,

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:38.960
how did you pitch the idea
to leadership to get them on board?

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:41.950
I know you just mentioned
that the CEO was there for the

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:43.390
beginning of the competition.

00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:47.875
What did you appeal to in order
to get them to buy into the idea?

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:51.130
- Yes, we didn't ask.

00:15:54.376 --> 00:15:56.650
We threw it out there. We
threw it out there and said--

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:59.740
I saw another question about did
we start with a problem and

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:00.970
have people solve the problem?

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:02.860
We just threw a data set
out there and said, look,

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:05.230
find some insights. We
left it really broad.

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:06.550
People came back
and said, well,

00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:08.740
how much time should I spend
on this? And, of course,

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:11.720
data scientists can go nuts
on something like this.

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:12.520
So we tried to,

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:17.155
look, you only have three total
weeks to work on this thing,

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:21.670
and you have a big broad
team and everything.

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:24.460
So for the question
that was just posed.

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:26.050
We didn't ask leadership.

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:28.060
We asked people to volunteer

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:29.940
and

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:31.500
just--

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:35.980
try to keep it manageable. I
think I forget what we said,

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:38.800
total number of hours over
the three weeks, maybe around

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:40.330
8 to 12.

00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:41.890
- I think we said it was
up to their discretion.

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:43.990
- It's up to their
discretion. Some did a lot,

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:46.410
but some did not so much.

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:49.070
- I think part of it,
too, was there was support for this.

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:52.250
I think we've been going
through a lot of changes, and

00:16:52.250 --> 00:16:54.890
again, there was a lot of focus
on data. So part of this, too,

00:16:54.890 --> 00:16:55.890
was a culture piece,

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:59.930
and I think the recognition that
this is a vital part of

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:02.910
working at a company is
the culture and the experience.

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:06.410
So I think a lot of that
also allowed for this to happen and

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:09.095
not really need
permission per se.

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:15.020
- I see Ted commented about
the data governance piece being so important,

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:19.520
and maybe this shifts a little
bit away from the Data Jam. But

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:23.510
what are some things
you've learned about data governance

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:24.890
through this or before?

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:26.990
- Yeah, this is a good
one. I can take this.

00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:31.670
My role here is around
data stewardship.

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:34.520
People talk about data
governance, but personally,

00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:37.970
everyone hates data governance
across the board, executives,

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:41.520
everyone. So I rebranded
it into data stewardship,

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:44.630
which I think evokes a little
bit more of we're all in this

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:48.140
together, and we do our own
stewardship of the information.

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:51.990
So what we ask people to do,
and I'm going to get it wrong,

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.080
what we asked. But we
asked for like an insight,

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:56.900
and we asked for a data
stewardship recommendation,

00:17:56.900 --> 00:17:58.370
and we asked for
one other thing,

00:17:58.370 --> 00:18:00.370
but I forget what that was now.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:03.530
But we got a lot of stuff.

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:06.725
And then, the survey itself
was about how well does Biogen

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:11.010
steward data and how
well do we leverage data.

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:13.160
So all of the questions in
the survey were about this,

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:14.885
and then the topic
was about this, too.

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:19.490
So like what we heard was
probably not surprising to a

00:18:19.490 --> 00:18:20.960
lot of people, but
data is hard to find.

00:18:21.950 --> 00:18:23.120
It's hard to get
access to data.

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:27.200
And then we had a lot
of great recommendations on how we could

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:29.360
improve that. Anuja,
did I miss anything?

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:30.910
- No,

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:34.850
you covered it pretty well. But I
think, in addition to that, the fact that

00:18:36.050 --> 00:18:40.130
I would say 80% to 90%
of the teams, which were incredibly

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:44.810
cross-functional, came to the
same conclusion, which was that

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:46.460
data is hard to
find and access.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:48.860
And so it's not
a problem that's

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:53.500
isolated to a single
group. It's pretty

00:18:55.640 --> 00:18:59.030
cross-functional, which I thought
was fascinating. It was expected,

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:02.330
but it was still very
interesting to actually see it.

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:03.500
- I think part of it, too,

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:05.975
generated a lot of ideas
on how to make it better.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:09.890
And so that was some of the
pitches had geared their

00:19:09.890 --> 00:19:12.530
thoughts and their pitch around, OK,
yeah, this is what it is.

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:16.610
But these are some of the ways
that we could work to improve it.

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:16.715
-

00:19:16.715 --> 00:19:19.760
I'd love to hear some of
those ways to improve it because I'm

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:21.030
sure a lot of us are facing

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:23.600
that, too, if that's
something you can share.

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:25.955
- I know there was a lot around

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:28.880
kind of metadata-driven
cataloging,

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:31.830
ensuring that your data
isn't all over the place.

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:34.940
There was a lot of conversation
about the fact that

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:36.940
copying

00:19:37.460 --> 00:19:40.760
data is something that happens
on occasion is and is obviously

00:19:40.760 --> 00:19:41.873
not best practice.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:43.100
So

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:48.110
I think the big ones that
we saw were essentially a catalog

00:19:48.110 --> 00:19:50.645
to understand what
data even exists

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:54.200
so that we know what's there,

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:57.860
and then find a way for people
who need to access it to be

00:19:57.860 --> 00:19:58.835
able to access it.

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:01.835
So that was a big
one that we saw.

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:02.540
-

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:04.280
And the other piece really
was actually having an

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:05.870
enterprise-wide strategy.

00:20:06.590 --> 00:20:10.190
I think that was one of
the key things that was identified was

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:13.250
just needing some consistent
across enterprise and not just

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:16.490
specific to each of the
different individual functions.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:18.800
- I'm trying to think
back, too. This was in May,

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:20.660
so it was a little bit ago, but

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:25.030
one of the ones that
resonated with me was not

00:20:26.030 --> 00:20:26.530
to

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:32.060
create a partial catalog or
something like that where as

00:20:32.060 --> 00:20:33.410
soon as you create
it. It's out of,

00:20:33.410 --> 00:20:37.110
date and then it ends up
being another stale data asset that

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:40.560
you have, so really trying
to way, like Danielle was saying,

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.600
to enterprise it or

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:46.080
productionalize it and then find
ways to make sure that

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:49.230
it's sustainable long term because
I feel like that's where

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:53.190
we always end up with
a hiccup of how do we--

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:54.150
we could get it going,

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:56.355
but then how do we
actually keep it going?

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:03.810
- Is that your
team that's responsible for enterprise

00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:05.595
data strategy?

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:08.370
I'm trying to remember the
name of the group Technology

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:11.565
Strategy and Innovation or
is that another [INAUDIBLE] in

00:21:11.565 --> 00:21:12.490
a company?

00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:14.010
- No, mine's like--

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:16.980
the piece that I'm in charge
of is the clinical trial data.

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:19.140
So obviously, at
Biogen, there's more

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:22.440
data than that. But
the enterprise--

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:25.800
it's just across all the
individual functions that they

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:28.590
do. Probably similar to
what it looks like at Posit,

00:21:28.590 --> 00:21:31.005
probably similar to what it
looks like at everyone else's company.

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:34.950
- Yeah, there was an
anonymous question that was,

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:37.650
"from technology perspective,

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:40.290
how did teams interact
on the Data Jam?

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:41.670
Did they use common tools?"

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:45.173
- There is a variety.

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:46.976
And I think the

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:48.940
randomization of teams

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:53.380
spoke to what tools ended
up being used. So for instance--

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:57.010
We obviously didn't interact
with every single team.

00:21:57.010 --> 00:22:00.595
But what ended up
happening was there was a

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:02.860
data communication person,

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:05.440
a person in charge of
creating the output,

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:07.690
a person who was more
of the programmer,

00:22:07.690 --> 00:22:09.190
and then the person who was

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:14.030
describing what the approach would
be to actually solving the problem.

00:22:14.030 --> 00:22:18.070
So it really depended on
what programming tools the people

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:20.870
within that specific team
were most comfortable with,

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:23.140
which kind of made it more
exciting because we had

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:26.200
people working in R,
people working in SAS,

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:27.550
people working in Power BI,

00:22:28.300 --> 00:22:29.755
amongst others. And so

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:31.900
folks did rely

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:34.740
a lot on what we had in-house

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:36.670
just because it's

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:41.690
sensitive data, so we didn't want
to put it on external tools.

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:46.760
But there was still quite a variety
of what was used to produce the

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:49.550
end results and the solutions.

00:22:52.310 --> 00:22:53.530
- Thank you. George,

00:22:53.530 --> 00:22:56.620
I see you had a question
in the chat just now,

00:22:56.620 --> 00:22:58.160
but maybe also
one from earlier.

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:00.160
So feel free to ask both there.

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:02.780
- Thanks. Yeah,

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:05.390
I was just wondering if
you obtained any feedback from the

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:09.740
participants on the different
aspects like the technology

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:11.290
side, what [INAUDIBLE],

00:23:13.190 --> 00:23:14.420
especially [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:14.420 --> 00:23:17.960
they may not really interact
with a lot [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:19.290
cross-functionally.

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:20.990
- Anija or Danielle, do
you want to take that?

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:24.650
- Yeah, so we did actually
do a post-event survey.

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:27.380
So a lot of
it was just understanding thoughts

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:29.780
and feedback on the format.

00:23:30.950 --> 00:23:32.840
Would they participate in
something like this again?

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:34.170
I think at the end of the day,

00:23:34.170 --> 00:23:36.710
there was a lot of really
positive feedback on the actual

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:38.750
Data Jam and wanting
to do it again.

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:41.510
And there were just obviously
some logistical things that we

00:23:41.510 --> 00:23:44.780
had to deal with that we
could always make better for next time.

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:48.560
I think one of the
challenges was the different geographic locations.

00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:52.310
I think that it was tough
trying to find teams that were

00:23:52.310 --> 00:23:55.010
spread across when they could
actually meet because of the

00:23:55.010 --> 00:23:56.135
different time zones. So

00:23:56.870 --> 00:23:59.810
there were a lot of things that
we have on our list to think

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:03.810
about for Data Jam 2.0. But at the end,

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:06.360
we did get a lot of
positive feedback and a lot of good

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:07.590
ideas for the next one.

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:11.030
I don't know Anuja,
Dan, anything to add?

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:12.890
- No, I don't. Actually,

00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:16.085
don't think we got too
much feedback in the way of

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:20.105
usage of tools. The
technology piece actually

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:22.190
seemed to work pretty well,

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:24.500
depending on what people wanted
to use. To Danielle's point,

00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:26.450
it was more
logistics. And then,

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:29.330
there was also feedback around the
type of data set that we

00:24:29.330 --> 00:24:32.450
could potentially use
for upcoming Data Jams.

00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:35.120
So for instance, for
this one, we did surveys,

00:24:35.120 --> 00:24:39.550
and some people love analyzing
survey data. Some people despise it.

00:24:40.250 --> 00:24:43.850
So there were some feedback
there around what else can we

00:24:43.850 --> 00:24:46.475
do moving forward and what
data sets could we use.

00:24:47.540 --> 00:24:48.630
- I think that was
another good point.

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:50.130
A lot of people had
offered like, hey,

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:52.310
are we going to do this
again? Can you please use our data?

00:24:52.310 --> 00:24:53.705
So that was pretty cool, too.

00:24:54.470 --> 00:24:55.700
- Yeah, that's exciting.

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:58.340
Now you're getting people
thinking about, hey,

00:24:58.340 --> 00:24:59.870
want to use my data for this.

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:05.960
I see there are a few questions
in the chat here, too. I think,

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:07.655
Kevin, you asked
one about different

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:10.900
formats that people--

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:11.590
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:12.230
- Yeah.

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:13.580
- Yeah, I like that idea

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:17.690
of forcing them not
to use PowerPoint

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:22.490
because I definitely
find PowerPoint presentation--

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:25.070
I definitely learn less
from PowerPoint presentations than

00:25:25.070 --> 00:25:27.050
any other format, I
think, that exists.

00:25:27.680 --> 00:25:31.060
But I'm curious what other
formats people ended up using instead

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:32.570
of PowerPoint.

00:25:32.570 --> 00:25:32.660
-

00:25:32.660 --> 00:25:36.710
So some people use the Power
BI dashboards directly and went through that.

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:40.340
Others use some forms of
like Canva or animations.

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:43.940
There was one really cool
group that did these animations to

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:44.675
tell their story.

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:50.610
Anything? I know. I so a
lot of Power BI, Canva, animations.

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:53.200
- There were a couple
of shiny applications.

00:25:53.200 --> 00:25:53.610
- Oh, there you go.

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:53.940
- Shiny--

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:54.210
- Yeah.

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:54.710
- Go ahead.

00:25:55.980 --> 00:25:56.480
- There were--

00:25:57.330 --> 00:25:59.220
so when you put this
question out there.

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:01.590
You never know what you're
going to get. You have to come

00:26:01.590 --> 00:26:04.800
back. It has to be a
presentation under five

00:26:04.800 --> 00:26:05.970
minutes, I think?

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:06.360
- Yep.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:08.700
- Five minutes to explain
these three things.

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:13.210
So what are we going to
get? It was unbelievable.

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:15.220
We had people like
dressing up in costumes.

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:21.750
We had people do a set
of trivia questions.

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:23.085
- Or they played the Kahoot.

00:26:23.670 --> 00:26:26.760
- And then we definitely
have some animation

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:28.590
to tell the whole story,

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:33.140
people walking through
the Power BI dashboard,

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:36.200
people walking through
shiny apps, stuff like that.

00:26:36.200 --> 00:26:38.090
But some really
interesting stuff.

00:26:38.090 --> 00:26:39.320
And I think people
had fun with it,

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:40.715
even if they didn't have a big

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:43.970
epiphany of data
science in this.

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:46.730
But they were just able
to present something because we know

00:26:47.930 --> 00:26:49.880
you need a lot of
skills for data science.

00:26:49.880 --> 00:26:53.360
And so part of the storytelling
and everything is part of it to

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:54.680
getting the message across.

00:26:55.400 --> 00:26:57.590
- So which formats do you
think were most effective?

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:00.590
- I would say the
funnier, the better

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:03.680
personally for this one.

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:08.345
But I think it really depends
on what message you're trying to convey.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:09.890
If you're really--

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:11.585
I think we have some people
who did some really deep

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:15.360
statistical analyzes, and
they had shiny apps up,

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:18.590
and I think that was really helpful
to do that stuff. But to have--

00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:22.400
for the big, broad
300-person audience,

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:24.110
I think you want
to go big and go

00:27:25.370 --> 00:27:25.870
creative.

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:28.040
- Yeah, I think when we
think about the finale,

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:29.060
there were a lot of--

00:27:29.060 --> 00:27:30.260
there are a few that
were powered BI,

00:27:30.260 --> 00:27:32.870
but they didn't just do
Power BI. They had some

00:27:33.690 --> 00:27:36.690
animations or the way they had
presented it was a little bit

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:38.085
on the more creative side.

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:42.180
- Yeah, I had this idea of

00:27:44.148 --> 00:27:47.940
just making it accessible to
whatever tools people wanted to

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:51.210
use. If they wanted to
present it in Canva,

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:54.120
which is a tool I love
for doing design, too,

00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:57.165
that they could use whatever they wanted.

00:27:58.660 --> 00:27:59.830
- Yeah, and feel like--

00:27:59.830 --> 00:28:00.770
- Go ahead.

00:28:00.770 --> 00:28:01.600
- Oh, sorry, Dan.

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:03.370
- I was just going to
say it was less about

00:28:04.810 --> 00:28:05.860
the output,

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:09.580
like one type of output
being more effective than the other,

00:28:09.580 --> 00:28:12.700
but more so about how
you communicated what you were

00:28:12.700 --> 00:28:16.400
trying to get at,
how you communicated your story.

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:21.420
So I feel like I
saw effective presentations across all formats.

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:22.720
-

00:28:22.720 --> 00:28:25.270
And I'm just going to say
we're trying to make it as inclusive

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:27.535
as possible. So
it's not only for

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:32.740
PhD statisticians, of which we
have a lot here. It's really--

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:35.590
it could be a novice and
have no idea anything about data,

00:28:36.550 --> 00:28:39.520
but you'd be maybe more
creative for the presentation

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:41.740
side, and you could
hear from them,

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:44.185
so a lot of cross-pollination
with different people

00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:47.390
and really keeping it open,
use whatever you want,

00:28:47.390 --> 00:28:48.265
do whatever you want.

00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:51.400
- Again, I think the teams
also really leveraged the

00:28:52.120 --> 00:28:54.350
skill sets and the strengths
of each member on their team.

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:56.350
So some of the people were
more heavy in the background,

00:28:56.350 --> 00:28:58.840
and some of the other people
were more front and center in

00:28:58.840 --> 00:28:59.870
terms of presentation.

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:02.890
So I think a lot of teams
use that to their advantage as well.

00:29:05.280 --> 00:29:07.260
- That's really cool. Thanks.
Thanks for sharing that.

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:11.040
George, you'd like a
follow-up on that kind of--

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:13.455
- Yeah, actually,

00:29:14.310 --> 00:29:16.720
I was wondering if
any of those creative

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:18.800
formats

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:22.350
have become normalized in
the culture because that's--

00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:24.180
it seems like such a variety.

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:27.390
I don't expect someone
to just constantly dance,

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:30.090
do an interpretive dance
to their presentations,

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:32.820
but something along
the lines of--

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:36.350
a little bit more creativity
and going beyond the PowerPoint.

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:39.420
Have any of those
become a mainstay?

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:43.830
- Speaking personally, now, when
I try to do something,

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:46.500
I think about what can I
use other than PowerPoint.

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:48.310
I don't know if that's
necessarily across the board,

00:29:48.310 --> 00:29:50.040
but it's definitely
something that I keep in mind

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:54.000
anytime I'm trying to present
some sort of visualization or

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:56.460
insights, is trying to be
a little bit more creative.

00:29:57.580 --> 00:29:57.790
- Yeah.

00:29:57.790 --> 00:30:00.130
- I think we definitely got
some feedback saying, oh, I'm going to

00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:03.775
have to rethink about how
I've been presenting analytics results.

00:30:04.840 --> 00:30:07.900
And our CEO is
definitely anti-PowerPoint, too. So

00:30:10.015 --> 00:30:11.507
he likes the idea of

00:30:12.970 --> 00:30:15.790
information being communicated in
some other way that's more--

00:30:15.790 --> 00:30:17.960
like other people are
saying here, it's more accessible,

00:30:17.960 --> 00:30:19.990
more easier to interpret.

00:30:21.280 --> 00:30:23.170
- I'm feeling bad
for PowerPoint right now.

00:30:23.170 --> 00:30:25.000
Some PowerPoints
are beautiful, too.

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:30.080
I see, Alan. You had a
question a little bit earlier.

00:30:30.080 --> 00:30:30.880
Do you want to jump in here?

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:33.080
ALAN: Yeah, sure.

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:35.390
I was curious if there
were insights or things in

00:30:35.390 --> 00:30:38.300
particular from what people
produced that could be pulled

00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:42.860
back by the owners of the data
to turn into anything that

00:30:42.860 --> 00:30:45.500
becomes programmatic or insights that
they can then act on.

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:49.470
That obviously would be a great
outcome like, hey, we get 100,

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:50.885
200 people together, and

00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:53.990
it's a great engaging activity,

00:30:53.990 --> 00:30:57.360
and it's super cool if the
team that owns that data could say,

00:30:57.360 --> 00:30:58.520
hey, you found a
bunch of stuff,

00:30:58.520 --> 00:31:02.060
or you started some ideas that
we can capitalize on and take forward.

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:04.805
Were they able to take
anything like that away?

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:07.860
- We'll let you,
Dan, speak about it.

00:31:10.410 --> 00:31:12.630
- So I think a lot of data gems

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:15.330
go with this idea of--

00:31:16.290 --> 00:31:19.230
here's this data set, or
maybe like Kaggle's like this,

00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:21.840
right here's this data set,
figure out this thing for me.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:23.770
And ours wasn't
exactly like that.

00:31:23.770 --> 00:31:26.250
So we didn't know exactly
what might come out of it.

00:31:26.250 --> 00:31:28.170
But I can tell you
what came out of it,

00:31:28.170 --> 00:31:30.510
at least from my perspective.

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:33.000
I know a lot of people reached
out to some of the people who

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:38.475
were either shiny developers or Power BI
to get consults on how to do stuff.

00:31:40.290 --> 00:31:42.060
Our CEO reached
out to us and asked

00:31:42.810 --> 00:31:47.400
how do we fix this whole
data problem that we have at Biogen

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:49.965
that he just heard for
two hours during the Data Jam.

00:31:51.870 --> 00:31:54.060
And I had one more,
but then I lost it.

00:31:55.160 --> 00:31:58.910
Oh, and one of
the metadata catalogs that someone

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:03.470
recommended, they called it DASH.
I forget what it stood for,

00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:07.350
but it was an idea of
just a metadata catalog.

00:32:07.350 --> 00:32:12.570
And that's being taken up
by our computational biology in research,

00:32:12.570 --> 00:32:14.940
and they're going to try
to push that forward next year.

00:32:15.750 --> 00:32:15.900
-

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:19.275
Maybe this isn't something you can
necessarily put a number around, but

00:32:20.260 --> 00:32:22.585
have you seen that
people are working

00:32:23.290 --> 00:32:26.710
better cross-functionally because
of the Data Jam?

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:31.260
- Biogen is a big company.

00:32:32.610 --> 00:32:34.395
I think we have
about 7,000 employees

00:32:35.670 --> 00:32:37.350
worldwide. So I don't
know that we can

00:32:38.010 --> 00:32:41.265
really get a pure
KPI on that one.

00:32:42.160 --> 00:32:44.970
But I think you
definitely hear of some

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:48.322
interactions that never would
have taken place before.

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:52.540
And I think we've run these
two community of practices,

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:53.700
which I think are like this,

00:32:53.700 --> 00:32:56.250
where you get people who
are passionate about something together.

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:57.375
And you have them talk about

00:32:58.110 --> 00:32:59.050
different topics.

00:32:59.050 --> 00:33:02.520
We have one around data stewardship
that I run and one about

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:07.510
Biogen analytics and data
community of practice,

00:33:07.510 --> 00:33:08.730
which they call Bad Cop.

00:33:08.730 --> 00:33:09.340
- Bad cop.

00:33:09.340 --> 00:33:12.010
- --which Danielle
and Anuja run.

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:15.580
But we've definitely had
more people volunteer for leadership

00:33:15.580 --> 00:33:17.515
in there, and we're
getting their voices in

00:33:18.790 --> 00:33:20.560
to continue this conversation.

00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:24.160
- I can say too that I've
gotten a lot of feedback on,

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:25.750
oh, thank you so
much for having this.

00:33:25.750 --> 00:33:27.730
I was able to connect
with this person,

00:33:27.730 --> 00:33:30.190
and now was able to
solve whatever issue or whatever

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:33.200
problem, or I was able to
get extra help on this.

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:37.480
So there has been a lot of
connections made that then help

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:40.150
somebody in their current
role or responsibility.

00:33:41.100 --> 00:33:41.600
- That's great.

00:33:42.820 --> 00:33:45.830
OK, Kevin, with the hard
question in the chat.

00:33:45.830 --> 00:33:47.590
Do you want to jump
in here, Kevin?

00:33:47.590 --> 00:33:47.680
-

00:33:47.680 --> 00:33:50.110
You said your CEO asked you
how do you fix the whole data

00:33:50.110 --> 00:33:51.820
problem. I'm just
curious, what did you say,

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:54.700
or what are you planning on
getting back to him with an

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:57.265
answer for that question?
That's a big question.

00:33:58.210 --> 00:34:00.850
- It was a big question.
And we were like, OK, yeah,

00:34:01.870 --> 00:34:02.890
we'll get back to you.

00:34:03.590 --> 00:34:04.960
We thought about
it for a long time.

00:34:06.430 --> 00:34:06.930
And

00:34:07.480 --> 00:34:09.230
I think we've seen,
I'm sure you've seen,

00:34:09.230 --> 00:34:11.110
I'm not sure what
organization you're in, but

00:34:11.980 --> 00:34:16.510
a lot of these initiatives come and
go, and a lot of hype about

00:34:16.510 --> 00:34:20.929
fix the world through data or
fix the entire business through data.

00:34:20.929 --> 00:34:22.584
So we talked about this a lot,

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:25.525
Danielle, Anuja, myself, Sam.

00:34:26.530 --> 00:34:27.030
And

00:34:28.060 --> 00:34:29.560
the idea--

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:32.665
everyone wants to answer
to this one. OK, well,

00:34:33.190 --> 00:34:35.410
the answer is it's complicated.

00:34:36.760 --> 00:34:40.360
But I think the answer that we
really need to do is where do

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:43.385
we want to be as an
organization with data.

00:34:44.120 --> 00:34:46.070
And we need to start from there

00:34:46.850 --> 00:34:49.340
and then go down through

00:34:50.449 --> 00:34:53.150
exactly how do we want
to improve our business,

00:34:53.150 --> 00:34:56.150
and then try to connect
our data scientists,

00:34:56.150 --> 00:35:01.400
our data innovators, into those
real business decisions or

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:02.864
business questions
that are there.

00:35:04.520 --> 00:35:06.830
So that's the my
five-minute Shark

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:08.600
Tank pitch on
what you need to do.

00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:10.790
You need to align the business.
You need to get them--

00:35:13.640 --> 00:35:16.010
state their state
their position on data,

00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:17.180
because otherwise
people like us,

00:35:17.180 --> 00:35:19.490
we always just like
we do our initiatives,

00:35:19.490 --> 00:35:22.790
we try to do our things and
it all hits some glass ceiling at

00:35:22.790 --> 00:35:26.900
some point, which we're all too
familiar with. I don't know, Danielle, Anuja?

00:35:26.900 --> 00:35:28.205
- I was just going
to say, I think,

00:35:28.205 --> 00:35:30.060
the one of the things
that we had recommended,

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:33.000
and so we had worked on
putting this proposal together,

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:36.410
which is very inclusive of
what Dan was talking about.

00:35:36.410 --> 00:35:38.900
But I think the first thing
was really just actually having an

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:43.620
enterprise-wide data strategy goal.
And it's something on

00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:47.540
as a goal for our metrics to
at least have some traction

00:35:47.540 --> 00:35:49.685
against at that
enterprise-wide level.

00:35:50.270 --> 00:35:53.720
- One of the anonymous questions
from a little bit earlier was

00:35:54.800 --> 00:35:57.860
what did you think about
the follow-through?

00:35:57.860 --> 00:35:59.280
Are you collecting
and resharing

00:36:00.020 --> 00:36:01.490
people's sharings?

00:36:01.490 --> 00:36:03.860
Is there a follow-through
to hold another event?

00:36:03.860 --> 00:36:08.780
- We did a couple of
things at the end to essentially make

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:11.660
people's solutions
accessible to all of Biogen.

00:36:11.660 --> 00:36:13.010
So just for some context,

00:36:13.010 --> 00:36:16.790
the finale following all the
battle rounds was an open

00:36:16.790 --> 00:36:17.910
invite to everyone.

00:36:17.910 --> 00:36:20.690
And so we had a
number of people join from various

00:36:20.690 --> 00:36:22.760
different departments
who weren't necessarily

00:36:23.420 --> 00:36:25.640
participating in the Data Jam.

00:36:25.640 --> 00:36:29.300
And so one thing we
did was collect links to everyone's

00:36:29.300 --> 00:36:31.370
Power BIs, and dashboards,

00:36:31.370 --> 00:36:34.910
and shiny applications and
final findings and put them

00:36:35.570 --> 00:36:38.870
in an accessible location
for everyone to view and see.

00:36:40.250 --> 00:36:41.840
Again, because it
was a five-minute Shark

00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:42.650
Tank pitch,

00:36:42.650 --> 00:36:45.120
we couldn't necessarily
delve into the details.

00:36:45.120 --> 00:36:47.810
And so this gave people
the opportunity to look into how

00:36:47.810 --> 00:36:51.620
people did the analysis and
what the more detailed findings were.

00:36:51.620 --> 00:36:53.255
So that's one
thing that we did.

00:36:53.960 --> 00:36:56.960
We would love to hold another
data Jam because this one has

00:36:56.960 --> 00:37:00.680
been so successful. We haven't necessarily
planned for one yet, but we're,

00:37:01.340 --> 00:37:03.830
I think, still
hoping to do one. But

00:37:06.170 --> 00:37:09.050
what has happened is,
I think Danielle mentioned earlier,

