there were any questions that we missed.

00:51:58.960 --> 00:52:01.420
Something I was curious about
when you were talking about the

00:52:01.420 --> 00:52:04.525
different community events
and people who may be are

00:52:05.410 --> 00:52:07.165
new to data science as well,

00:52:08.420 --> 00:52:11.630
what does the training
look like for your team?

00:52:11.630 --> 00:52:12.925
So if somebody who maybe--

00:52:14.080 --> 00:52:16.060
I guess there's
two factors here,

00:52:16.060 --> 00:52:18.910
onboarding for someone who
comes in with data science

00:52:18.910 --> 00:52:22.210
experience or analytics
experience versus also somebody

00:52:22.210 --> 00:52:26.260
who maybe hadn't used data
science before but wants to.

00:52:26.260 --> 00:52:26.785
- Maybe Danielle.

00:52:28.660 --> 00:52:31.210
- So a lot of it really
is on-the-job training.

00:52:31.970 --> 00:52:35.170
So it's really just once
you working with the team working

00:52:35.170 --> 00:52:35.935
with your manager.

00:52:36.700 --> 00:52:40.450
But at the same time, too, I
know for some of what we hire

00:52:40.450 --> 00:52:42.820
for, we try to be a
little bit diverse.

00:52:42.820 --> 00:52:43.990
So a lot of times,

00:52:43.990 --> 00:52:46.930
there are some technical
aspects that we are looking

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:51.620
for, but a lot of the times
for anything really data-related in my world.

00:52:51.620 --> 00:52:54.830
I look for people that are
just genuinely curious and want to

00:52:54.830 --> 00:52:56.850
solve problems. So I feel like

00:52:57.890 --> 00:53:00.440
as long as you have those
two skills and that you're willing

00:53:00.440 --> 00:53:02.820
to learn, that's
pretty much how

00:53:03.620 --> 00:53:04.685
we get things done.

00:53:05.330 --> 00:53:05.810
Don't know Dan--

00:53:05.810 --> 00:53:07.370
and in your space if
it's any different.

00:53:07.370 --> 00:53:10.160
But that's at least how
I handle things over here.

00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:13.130
- Yeah, I think in the
data science groups,

00:53:13.130 --> 00:53:15.155
we hire a lot of
more senior people.

00:53:15.920 --> 00:53:18.440
There's not a lot of
entry-level roles there.

00:53:18.440 --> 00:53:20.450
But I think maybe
you're also hinting at

00:53:21.260 --> 00:53:24.080
although I hate the
word, a data literacy

00:53:25.040 --> 00:53:26.420
effort of how you--

00:53:26.420 --> 00:53:30.920
how does every single person
in the entire organization know

00:53:30.920 --> 00:53:32.105
not to present a pie chart

00:53:33.980 --> 00:53:35.040
and stuff like that.

00:53:35.040 --> 00:53:36.770
And that's something
that we need to look at.

00:53:36.770 --> 00:53:38.870
And I think these community
practices that we have are the

00:53:38.870 --> 00:53:42.447
best way that we can really
do it. It's organic. It's leaning

00:53:43.910 --> 00:53:46.280
on people already
wanting to learn and then

00:53:47.120 --> 00:53:51.230
helping them get up to the next
level with what they already want to learn.

00:53:51.230 --> 00:53:54.130
- I know we're speaking to
the communities of practice,

00:53:54.130 --> 00:53:55.270
but there are some--

00:53:55.270 --> 00:53:58.030
ours are a little more
general, but there are specialized ones.

00:53:58.030 --> 00:53:59.455
So there's a machine
learning group

00:54:00.400 --> 00:54:02.860
that dives into that
a little bit more.

00:54:02.860 --> 00:54:06.530
And we're about to start
a data science conference

00:54:07.240 --> 00:54:10.520
which will bring in
speakers and such for

00:54:11.200 --> 00:54:14.110
people to learn a
little bit more there.

